WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.350 --> 00:00:02.014
Modern Architecture Styles.

2
00:00:02.294 --> 00:00:06.369
Chapter 34, from the Architect part of the System Design Playbook.

3
00:00:08.119 --> 00:00:09.079
At a glance:

4
00:00:09.359 --> 00:00:10.532
Chapter at a glance.

5
00:00:10.812 --> 00:00:11.815
Reader bands:

6
00:00:12.095 --> 00:00:14.377
B2, then B4, Prerequisites:

7
00:00:14.657 --> 00:00:19.948
Ch 16, Ch 18, Ch 31, Ch 33, What you will build:

8
00:00:20.228 --> 00:00:37.124
A working understanding of the architecture styles an architect actually chooses between, layered, hexagonal or clean, modular monolith, microservices, event-driven, serverless, micro-frontends, and cell-based, what each costs, and how to combine and evolve them.

9
00:00:37.404 --> 00:00:41.991
Four runnable labs measure the key trade-offs, including a real cell router.

10
00:00:43.341 --> 00:00:45.517
L1: hexagonal architecture.

11
00:00:45.797 --> 00:01:11.055
The same checkout domain ran 2,000 checkouts behind in-memory adapters and behind real ones (a file store plus a real HTTP payment gateway): identical outcomes (1,980 paid, 20 declined), the in-memory suite ~200 to 300x faster, and a reflection-based dependency check passed the domain and caught a class that took an HttpClient.

12
00:01:11.335 --> 00:01:13.639
L2: modules versus services.

13
00:01:13.919 --> 00:01:36.234
Five collaborator calls cost ~11 µs in-process, ~1.1 ms as serial HTTP calls on localhost (~99x) and ~0.5 ms fanned out; with six required 99.9% deployables, expected downtime rose from 43 to 259 minutes per month.

14
00:01:37.584 --> 00:01:39.845
L3: cells and blast radius.

15
00:01:40.125 --> 00:02:00.712
Over 10,000 simulated years, 8 cells with 90-minute deploy waves cut lost platform-minutes from 880 to 205 per year, but latent bugs still reached 100% of customers; spacing waves 12 h apart cut it to 110 per year and the worst incident to 12.5%.

16
00:02:00.992 --> 00:02:02.933
L4: a real cell router.

17
00:02:03.213 --> 00:02:21.091
Moving Pune between cells under live traffic: a naive routing flip returned zero errors and silently lost 1,519 acknowledged items; a hold, copy, flip migration lost 0, holding only Pune's requests for ~13 ms.

18
00:02:21.371 --> 00:02:22.352
Reading time:

19
00:02:22.632 --> 00:02:24.595
120 min + code.

20
00:02:25.945 --> 00:02:26.798
Part 1.

21
00:02:27.078 --> 00:02:28.998
Introduction and Why It Matters.

22
00:02:30.248 --> 00:02:32.893
A story: three architectures in three years.

23
00:02:33.173 --> 00:02:36.757
The grocery platform has lived through every fashionable style.

24
00:02:37.037 --> 00:02:38.936
Year 1, the layered monolith.

25
00:02:39.216 --> 00:02:43.419
A Spring Boot application with controllers, services and repositories.

26
00:02:43.699 --> 00:02:44.808
It shipped fast.

27
00:02:45.088 --> 00:02:57.205
By month ten, services called each other freely, the OrderService class was 4,000 lines, business rules lived in controllers and SQL, and a change to pricing broke delivery-slot booking.

28
00:02:57.485 --> 00:03:00.515
Tests needed a database and took 25 minutes.

29
00:03:01.865 --> 00:03:03.699
Year 2, microservices.

30
00:03:03.979 --> 00:03:10.550
The team split the monolith into 26 services by entity (Ch 33 §1.1).

31
00:03:10.830 --> 00:03:23.374
Each service was small, but checkout now made nine synchronous calls, p99 latency tripled, a promotions deployment could take down checkout, and local development meant running 14 containers.

32
00:03:23.654 --> 00:03:26.491
The monolith's coupling had become network coupling.

33
00:03:26.771 --> 00:03:28.691
Year 3, a festival outage.

34
00:03:28.971 --> 00:03:36.843
During a Diwali flash sale, one city's surge exhausted the shared Kafka cluster and the shared orders database connection pool.

35
00:03:37.123 --> 00:03:42.606
Every city went down together, a customer in Chennai couldn't check out because of a sale in Pune.

36
00:03:42.886 --> 00:03:50.545
The post-incident review asked a question no style had answered: why can one city's problem become every city's problem?

37
00:03:51.895 --> 00:03:55.905
The re-architecture that followed didn't pick a single fashionable style.

38
00:03:56.185 --> 00:03:58.553
It combined several, each where it paid off:

39
00:03:58.833 --> 00:04:07.431
Hexagonal architecture inside every deployable, so the domain is testable without infrastructure and adapters are swappable (L1).

40
00:04:07.711 --> 00:04:15.433
A modular monolith for Ordering and Catalog, where in-process calls are cheap and a transaction can span modules (L2).

41
00:04:15.713 --> 00:04:23.863
Separate services only where a real driver existed, Inventory and Fulfilment, which need independent scaling for flash sales.

42
00:04:24.143 --> 00:04:27.471
Events between contexts, not chains of synchronous calls.

43
00:04:28.821 --> 00:04:40.831
Cells, independent copies of the whole stack, each serving a group of cities, so that an infrastructure failure, a bad deploy or one city's surge is confined to one cell (L3, L4).

44
00:04:42.181 --> 00:04:42.864
Quote:

45
00:04:43.144 --> 00:04:44.808
The thesis of this chapter.

46
00:04:45.088 --> 00:04:51.040
An architecture style is a bundle of constraints you accept in exchange for specific qualities.

47
00:04:51.320 --> 00:05:11.075
Layered buys simplicity and costs coupling; hexagonal buys testability and replaceable infrastructure; modular monoliths buy cheap calls and simple operations; microservices buy independent deployment and scaling at the price of network calls and distributed failure; serverless buys zero-ops

48
00:05:11.355 --> 00:05:20.784
scaling at the price of cold starts and per-request cost; cells buy blast-radius containment at the price of duplicated infrastructure and routing.

49
00:05:21.064 --> 00:05:31.816
There is no best style, real systems combine them, one per level (inside a deployable, across deployables, across the fleet), and evolve between them as the drivers change.

50
00:05:33.166 --> 00:05:35.470
Where this lives in the grocery platform.

51
