98
00:07:02.449 --> 00:07:06.161
Invoice PDFs, image resizing: bursty, stateless.

99
00:07:06.441 --> 00:07:08.383
Lab: §2.7.

100
00:07:09.733 --> 00:07:11.055
Learning objectives.

101
00:07:11.335 --> 00:07:13.213
By the end you will be able to:

102
00:07:13.493 --> 00:07:18.570
Describe each major style by the constraints it imposes and the qualities it buys.

103
00:07:18.850 --> 00:07:23.501
Apply hexagonal architecture and enforce its dependency rule (L1).

104
00:07:23.781 --> 00:07:28.730
Price the move from modules to services in latency and availability (L2).

105
00:07:29.010 --> 00:07:37.095
Design a cell-based architecture, cell sizing, routing, deploy waves, control plane, and quantify blast radius (L3).

106
00:07:37.375 --> 00:07:40.362
Migrate tenants between cells safely (L4).

107
00:07:41.712 --> 00:07:45.083
Combine styles across levels and plan an evolution path.

108
00:07:46.433 --> 00:07:47.307
Part 2.

109
00:07:47.587 --> 00:07:49.486
Core Concepts, Beginner to Deep.

110
00:07:50.736 --> 00:07:53.445
Intuition: styles are bundles of constraints.

111
00:07:53.725 --> 00:08:02.685
An architecture style names a recurring set of structural decisions: what the building blocks are, how they may communicate, and what they may depend on.

112
00:08:02.965 --> 00:08:14.869
REST's constraints (statelessness, uniform interface) buy scalability and cacheability; the layered style's constraint (each layer uses only the layer below) buys separation of concerns.

113
00:08:15.149 --> 00:08:17.965
Every constraint closes some doors to open others.

114
00:08:18.245 --> 00:08:20.507
Three consequences shape this chapter:

115
00:08:20.787 --> 00:08:27.955
Choose by drivers, not by fashion (Ch 31): which quality attributes does this part of the system need most?

116
00:08:29.305 --> 00:08:31.161
Styles apply at different levels.

117
00:08:31.441 --> 00:08:41.638
Hexagonal describes the inside of a deployable; microservices describes how deployables relate; cell-based describes how copies of the whole system relate.

118
00:08:41.918 --> 00:08:45.374
You pick one at each level, they're not alternatives to each other.

119
00:08:45.654 --> 00:08:49.345
Styles are points on an evolution path, not permanent identities.

120
00:08:49.625 --> 00:08:59.139
Many successful systems go layered monolith, then modular monolith, then selective services, then cells, as scale and organization demand.

121
00:09:00.489 --> 00:09:02.409
Layered (n-tier) architecture.

122
00:09:02.689 --> 00:09:11.265
The default: presentation, then application or service, then domain, then persistence, each layer depending only on the one below.

123
00:09:11.545 --> 00:09:15.769
Buys: simplicity, familiarity, a clear place for each kind of code.

124
00:09:16.049 --> 00:09:33.927
Costs: the domain depends on persistence (the arrow points down to the database), so business logic tends to be written around tables; layers become pass-throughs (sinkhole anti-pattern); nothing stops a controller from calling a repository or one service from calling every other.

125
00:09:34.207 --> 00:09:37.833
Fits: small CRUD-heavy applications, early-stage products.

126
00:09:39.183 --> 00:09:45.455
The critical weakness is the direction of the dependency at the bottom: the domain knows about the database.

127
00:09:45.735 --> 00:09:48.509
Hexagonal architecture fixes exactly that.

128
00:09:49.859 --> 00:09:52.419
Hexagonal, onion and clean architecture.

129
00:09:52.699 --> 00:10:03.451
Alistair Cockburn's ports and adapters (hexagonal) architecture, and its close relatives Jeffrey Palermo's onion and Robert Martin's clean architecture, share one rule:

130
00:10:03.731 --> 00:10:05.352
Dependencies point inward.

131
00:10:05.632 --> 00:10:08.299
The domain is at the center and depends on nothing.

132
00:10:08.579 --> 00:10:11.757
Infrastructure depends on the domain, never the reverse.

133
00:10:12.037 --> 00:10:24.752
Ports are interfaces owned by the domain that describe what it needs (OrderRepository, PaymentPort), driven (secondary) ports, or what it offers (CheckoutUseCase), driving (primary) ports.

134
00:10:26.102 --> 00:10:40.502
Adapters implement ports for a technology: a JPA repository, a Razorpay HTTP client, a Kafka publisher (driven adapters); a REST controller, a Kafka listener, a CLI (driving adapters).

135
00:10:40.782 --> 00:10:46.094
Wiring (Spring configuration) connects adapters to ports at the edge of the application.

136
00:10:46.374 --> 00:10:48.401
What it buys (L1 measures both):

137
00:10:49.751 --> 00:10:52.033
Business rules testable at memory speed.

138
00:10:52.313 --> 00:11:05.988
L1 ran 2,000 checkouts through in-memory adapters in ~10 to 20 ms and through a real file store + HTTP gateway in ~3 s, with identical paid or declined outcomes.

139
00:11:06.268 --> 00:11:13.863
Most tests run against the fast wiring; each adapter gets a few contract or integration tests (Ch 29).

140
00:11:14.143 --> 00:11:15.807
Replaceable infrastructure.

141
00:11:16.087 --> 00:11:24.001
Moving from the file store to PostgreSQL, or from Razorpay to another gateway, is a new adapter; the domain doesn't change.

142
00:11:25.351 --> 00:11:33.757
The rule must be enforced, or it erodes the first time someone passes an HttpClient into a domain class for convenience.

143
00:11:34.037 --> 00:11:44.917
L1's reflection check (and ArchUnit in real projects, Ch 31 L2) is that fitness function, and L1 shows it catching exactly that shortcut.

144
00:11:46.267 --> 00:11:51.387
Here is the Java code, Package layout for one bounded context (hexagonal).

145
00:11:54.237 --> 00:12:05.287
Costs: more interfaces and mapping between domain objects and persistence or transport models; overkill for trivial CRUD (§2.11 of Ch 33).

146
00:12:06.637 --> 00:12:07.981
Modular monolith.

147
00:12:08.261 --> 00:12:25.243
A modular monolith is one deployable whose internals are divided into modules with enforced boundaries, typically one module per bounded context, that interact only through published module APIs or events, never by reaching into each other's internals or tables.

148
00:12:25.523 --> 00:12:47.539
Buys: in-process calls (L2: ~11 µs for five calls versus ~1.1 ms over HTTP on localhost, and far more in a real network), local ACID transactions when genuinely needed, one pipeline, simple debugging, simple local development, plus most of microservices' modularity benefits.

149
00:12:48.889 --> 00:13:01.091
Costs: one deploy unit (a bad release affects all modules, mitigate with canaries and feature flags); modules scale together; the boundaries must be enforced or it becomes a ball of mud.

150
00:13:01.371 --> 00:13:17.286
Enforcement: module packages with public API sub-packages; ArchUnit or Spring Modulith verification in CI; one schema (or table prefix) per module with no cross-module table access; events between modules.

151
00:13:18.636 --> 00:13:25.271
Here is the Java code, Spring Modulith verification (VERIFY: needs spring-modulith-starter-test).

152
00:13:28.121 --> 00:13:39.235
The modular monolith is the recommended default for most teams: it preserves the option to extract a module into a service later, cheaply, because the boundary is already clean.

153
00:13:40.585 --> 00:13:42.271
Microservices, priced.

154
00:13:42.551 --> 00:13:45.708
Ch 16 covers microservices in depth.

155
00:13:45.988 --> 00:13:49.679
The architectural point here is the price list, which L2 measures:

156
00:13:49.959 --> 00:14:02.801
Latency: each module-to-service boundary turns a microsecond call into a network call, ~99x on localhost in L2, before real network, TLS, sidecars and GC.

157
00:14:03.081 --> 00:14:05.812
Serial chains add; fan-out takes the max.

158
00:14:06.092 --> 00:14:13.303
Tail latency: p99 was already several milliseconds on localhost; every hop contributes its own tail.

159
00:14:14.653 --> 00:14:30.802
Availability: required synchronous dependencies multiply, six 99.9% deployables on a path give ~99.4%, that is, ~6x the downtime (43, then 259 min per month).

160
00:14:31.082 --> 00:14:36.970
Operations: per-service pipelines, observability, on-call, versioning and contract testing.

161
00:14:38.320 --> 00:14:50.608
What you buy with that price: independent deployment, independent scaling, fault isolation between services (if you remove synchronous coupling), technology freedom, and team autonomy at scale.

162
00:14:50.888 --> 00:15:07.784
The decision rule: extract a service when one of those benefits is concretely needed (a module with a different scaling profile, a different release cadence, a different team), and when you do, remove required synchronous hops, use events, caches and fan-out rather than chains.

163
00:15:09.134 --> 00:15:10.862
Event-driven architecture.

164
00:15:11.142 --> 00:15:20.507
In an event-driven style, components communicate by publishing and reacting to events rather than calling each other (Ch 14, Ch 15).

165
00:15:20.787 --> 00:15:21.918
Two topologies:

166
00:15:22.198 --> 00:15:27.894
Choreography, each service reacts to events and publishes its own; no central coordinator.

167
00:15:28.174 --> 00:15:32.761
Loosely coupled, but the end-to-end flow is implicit and harder to observe.

168
00:15:33.041 --> 00:15:40.081
Orchestration, a coordinator (saga orchestrator, workflow engine like Temporal) tells participants what to do.

169
00:15:40.361 --> 00:15:44.307
Explicit flow and easier error handling, but a central component.

170
00:15:45.657 --> 00:15:58.841
Event-driven styles buy temporal decoupling (the consumer can be down), natural fan-out, and an audit trail; they cost eventual consistency, harder debugging, and schema-evolution discipline (Ch 28).

171
00:15:59.121 --> 00:16:09.319
Most modern systems are hybrid: synchronous APIs for queries and commands that need an immediate answer, events for propagating facts between contexts.

172
00:16:10.669 --> 00:16:12.781
Serverless or Functions-as-a-Service.

173
00:16:13.061 --> 00:16:24.069
In serverless, you deploy functions (or containers) and the platform handles provisioning, scaling (including to zero) and patching; you pay per invocation and compute-time.

174
00:16:24.349 --> 00:16:32.434
Buys: no servers to manage, automatic scaling for spiky loads, zero cost when idle, fast delivery for event-driven glue.

175
00:16:33.784 --> 00:16:52.643
Costs: cold starts (hundreds of ms to seconds for JVM functions without SnapStart or native images), execution-time limits, statelessness (state goes to managed stores), vendor coupling, harder local testing, and a cost curve that grows linearly with volume with no ceiling,

176
00:16:52.923 --> 00:17:02.523
Ch 31 L4 showed serverless cheaper at the likely volume but more expensive in expectation once demand uncertainty is modeled.

177
00:17:02.803 --> 00:17:11.187
Fits: bursty, stateless, event-triggered work: invoice PDF generation, image resizing, webhook handling, scheduled jobs.

178
00:17:11.467 --> 00:17:16.864
For steady high-volume Java services, containers usually win on cost and latency.

179
00:17:18.214 --> 00:17:33.553
For Java specifically, cold starts are mitigated with GraalVM native images, AWS Lambda SnapStart or CRaC checkpointing, provisioned concurrency, or by running containers on a serverless container platform instead.

180
00:17:34.903 --> 00:17:36.460
Cell-based architecture.

181
00:17:36.740 --> 00:17:49.284
A cell is a complete, independent copy of the system's data plane, services, databases, caches, queues, serving a fixed subset of customers (for the grocery platform: a group of cities).

182
00:17:49.564 --> 00:17:56.540
A thin cell router maps each request to its cell; a control plane manages cells, routing tables and deployments.

183
00:17:56.820 --> 00:18:00.809
Cells share nothing at runtime except the router and the control plane.

184
00:18:01.089 --> 00:18:08.385
The purpose is blast-radius containment: cells are bulkheads (Ch 18) at the level of the whole architecture.

185
00:18:08.665 --> 00:18:12.143
L3 quantifies it over 10,000 simulated years:

186
00:18:13.493 --> 00:18:14.858
Here is a quick table:

187
00:18:15.138 --> 00:18:19.170
Design, and Platform-minutes lost or year, and Worst single incident.

188
00:18:19.450 --> 00:18:20.879
One global deployment.

189
00:18:21.159 --> 00:18:22.823
Platform-minutes lost or year:

190
00:18:23.103 --> 00:18:24.234
880.

191
00:18:24.514 --> 00:18:25.922
Worst single incident:

192
00:18:26.202 --> 00:18:28.037
100% of customers.

193
00:18:28.317 --> 00:18:30.749
8 cells, deploy waves over 90 min.

194
00:18:31.029 --> 00:18:32.693
Platform-minutes lost or year:

195
00:18:32.973 --> 00:18:34.125
205.

196
00:18:34.405 --> 00:18:35.813
Worst single incident:

197
00:18:36.093 --> 00:18:38.119
100% (latent bugs).

198
00:18:38.399 --> 00:18:41.066
8 cells, deploy waves 12 h apart.

199
00:18:41.346 --> 00:18:43.010
Platform-minutes lost or year:

200
00:18:43.290 --> 00:18:44.421
110.

201
00:18:44.701 --> 00:18:46.109
Worst single incident:

202
00:18:46.389 --> 00:18:47.903
12.5%.

203
00:18:49.253 --> 00:18:50.981
Two lessons hide in that table:

204
00:18:51.261 --> 00:19:01.288
Cells confine infrastructure failures and noisy neighbors (one city's flash sale, a poison tenant) to one cell, the dominant source of the 4x improvement.

205
00:19:02.638 --> 00:19:06.713
Cells don't help against latent bugs unless the deploy waves are slow.

206
00:19:06.993 --> 00:19:12.966
A defect that triggers hours after deploy has already reached every cell in a 90-minute rollout.

207
00:19:13.246 --> 00:19:20.521
Spacing waves (and per-cell feature flags) is what turns cells into protection against change as well as infrastructure.

208
00:19:20.801 --> 00:19:31.553
(L3's latent bugs trigger 2 to 10 h after deploy, so 12 h spacing catches all of them by construction; choose real bake times from your own incident history.).

209
00:19:31.833 --> 00:19:33.454
Design decisions for cells:

210
00:19:34.804 --> 00:19:36.553
Partition key and cell size.

211
00:19:36.833 --> 00:19:41.484
Choose a key with low cross-cell traffic (city group, tenant, customer ID).

212
00:19:41.764 --> 00:19:50.191
Size cells so a cell failure is tolerable (for example, at most 15% of customers) and so the largest tenant fits in one cell.

213
00:19:50.471 --> 00:19:53.713
Keep cells identical in shape so one runbook fits all.

214
00:19:53.993 --> 00:19:54.825
The router.

215
00:19:55.105 --> 00:20:00.332
Stateless, simple, highly available, with the routing table replicated to every edge.

216
00:20:00.612 --> 00:20:04.537
It must be the most reliable component in the system because it's shared.

217
00:20:05.887 --> 00:20:07.935
Control plane versus data plane.

218
00:20:08.215 --> 00:20:19.394
The control plane (provisioning, routing-table updates, deployment orchestration) can be less available than the data plane; cells must keep serving if the control plane is down.

219
00:20:19.674 --> 00:20:26.479
Deployments in waves, cell by cell, with a canary cell first and bake times sized to your latent-bug profile.

220
00:20:26.759 --> 00:20:28.082
Cross-cell concerns.

221
00:20:28.362 --> 00:20:44.042
National reporting (aggregate via a data platform, not live queries across cells), global uniqueness (IDs with a cell prefix, Ch 20), and customers or tenants that move between cells, which needs a migration protocol (L4).

222
00:20:45.392 --> 00:20:58.171
Migrating a tenant between cells (L4): a naive routing flip returned no errors and silently lost every acknowledged Pune item written before the flip, because the new cell had none of Pune's data.

223
00:20:58.451 --> 00:21:08.883
The safe protocol: hold the tenant's requests at the router (wait for in-flight ones to drain), copy the data, flip the route, release, then verify and drop the source.

224
00:21:09.163 --> 00:21:17.269
L4's hold lasted ~13 ms for a few thousand items and didn't touch Nagpur, another city on the same source cell.

225
00:21:17.549 --> 00:21:24.781
For large tenants, pre-copy with dual-write or CDC and pause only for the final delta (Ch 28).

226
00:21:26.131 --> 00:21:34.942
Cells cost duplicated infrastructure (8 copies of every database and cluster), more operational tooling, and cross-cell complexity.

227
00:21:35.222 --> 00:21:43.009
They pay off at large scale, millions of customers, many teams, strict availability targets, not for an early-stage product.

228
00:21:44.359 --> 00:21:45.916
Front-end architecture:

229
00:21:46.196 --> 00:21:48.180
BFF and micro-frontends.

230
00:21:48.460 --> 00:21:59.297
Backend for Frontend (BFF): a thin backend per client type (customer app, rider app, web, partner API) that aggregates and shapes data for that client.

231
00:21:59.577 --> 00:22:05.913
It keeps client-specific logic out of domain services and lets each client team evolve its API.

232
00:22:06.193 --> 00:22:08.220
Keep business rules out of BFFs.

233
00:22:09.570 --> 00:22:23.735
Micro-frontends: the front-end equivalent of microservices, a large web UI composed of independently built and deployed fragments owned by different teams (the seller portal's catalog, orders and payouts sections).

234
00:22:24.015 --> 00:22:29.007
Composition via module federation, web components or server-side includes.

235
00:22:29.287 --> 00:22:33.362
Costs: consistency of UX, bundle size, shared dependencies.

236
00:22:33.642 --> 00:22:39.594
Worth it only when several teams genuinely need to deploy parts of one UI independently.

237
00:22:40.944 --> 00:22:42.587
Data architecture styles.

238
00:22:42.867 --> 00:22:45.704
Styles apply to data too (Ch 21):

239
00:22:45.984 --> 00:22:56.565
Database per service or context, each context owns its data; others use APIs or events (the default with microservices and modular monoliths).

240
00:22:56.845 --> 00:23:01.773
Lambda or Kappa, batch + streaming versus streaming-only processing pipelines.

241
00:23:02.053 --> 00:23:07.749
Lakehouse, open table formats (Iceberg, Delta) over object storage for analytics.

242
00:23:09.099 --> 00:23:23.265
Data mesh, domain teams own and publish data products (with contracts, SLAs and discoverability) instead of a central data team owning all pipelines; a self-serve platform and federated governance make it work.

243
00:23:23.545 --> 00:23:32.334
Data mesh applies DDD's bounded-context thinking to analytical data; it's an organizational style as much as a technical one.

244
00:23:33.684 --> 00:23:35.561
Combining and evolving styles.

245
00:23:35.841 --> 00:23:38.060
Real architectures are compositions:

246
00:23:39.410 --> 00:23:40.775
