10,000 simulated. years each. architecture platform-min lost p95 year effective avail. worst incident. one global deployment 880 min 967 min 99.8325% 100.0%.

428
00:39:42.270 --> 00:39:52.062
8 cells, waves over 90 min 205 min 280 min 99.9611% 100.0%.

429
00:39:52.342 --> 00:40:02.070
8 cells, waves over 36 h 110 min 121 min 99.9791% 12.5%.

430
00:40:02.350 --> 00:40:12.035
8 cells, waves over 90 min: latent bugs had reached all 8 cells in 100% of cases;. mean share hit 100.0%.

431
00:40:12.315 --> 00:40:21.915
8 cells, waves over 36 h: latent bugs had reached all 8 cells in 0% of cases; mean. share hit 12.5%.

432
00:40:22.195 --> 00:40:23.710
The rest is in the chapter.

433
00:40:26.560 --> 00:40:27.413
Reading it:

434
00:40:27.693 --> 00:40:42.584
Cells cut expected impact ~4x (880, then 205 platform-minutes per year) because infrastructure failures and one city's surge, the §1.1 festival outage, are confined to one cell.

435
00:40:42.864 --> 00:40:51.952
Fast waves don't contain latent bugs: every latent bug had reached all 8 cells when it triggered, so the worst incident is still 100%.

436
00:40:53.302 --> 00:40:57.121
12-hour waves turn latent bugs into one-cell incidents too:

437
00:40:57.401 --> 00:41:02.627
110 platform-minutes per year and a worst incident of 12.5%.

438
00:41:02.907 --> 00:41:17.542
That's partly by construction, the model's latent bugs trigger within 10 hours, so real bake times must come from your own incident data; the structural lesson (wave spacing must exceed bug-manifestation time) holds regardless.

439
00:41:17.822 --> 00:41:28.233
The cost side isn't in this model: duplicated infrastructure, router, control plane, slower delivery for risky changes. §4's design doc prices it.

440
00:41:29.583 --> 00:41:33.316
L4, A real cell router, and moving a city between cells.

441
00:41:33.596 --> 00:41:34.535
Requirement:

442
00:41:34.815 --> 00:41:35.967
Cell 1 is hot.

443
00:41:36.247 --> 00:41:41.772
Move Pune to cell 3 during business hours without losing a single acknowledged cart item.

444
00:41:42.052 --> 00:41:49.455
Three cells are real HTTP servers with their own stores; a router maps city, then cell and proxies requests.

445
00:41:49.735 --> 00:41:54.172
Four Pune clients and two Nagpur clients add cart items continuously.

446
00:41:54.452 --> 00:42:03.305
After 1.5 s, Pune moves from cell 1 to cell 3, naively (flip the route) and properly (hold, copy, flip, release, drop).

447
00:42:03.585 --> 00:42:06.807
Every acknowledged item is then read back through the router.

448
00:42:07.087 --> 00:42:10.329
A silent warm-up run precedes the two reported runs.

449
00:42:11.679 --> 00:42:14.901
Here is the Java code, CellRouterL4.java.

450
00:42:15.181 --> 00:42:19.426
It runs to 248 lines; the full listing is in the chapter.

451
00:42:19.706 --> 00:42:31.311
CellRouterL4.java, run with: java -Dsun.net.httpserver.nodelay=true CellRouterL4.java (JDK 21, no dependencies).

452
00:42:31.591 --> 00:42:35.751
A REAL CELL ROUTER, and moving a city between cells under live traffic.

453
00:42:36.031 --> 00:42:43.775
Three cells are separate HTTP servers, each with. its own cart store (no shared database, that's the point of cells).

454
00:42:44.055 --> 00:42:47.319
A thin routing layer maps city, then cell and proxies.

455
00:42:50.169 --> 00:42:51.087
On screen:

456
00:42:51.367 --> 00:42:54.332
Cell router over 3 real HTTP cells.

457
00:42:54.612 --> 00:42:57.599
Pune + Nagpur on cell-1, Delhi on cell-2.

458
00:42:57.879 --> 00:42:59.073
4 Pune +.

459
00:42:59.353 --> 00:43:05.327
2 Nagpur clients add items for 3 s;. at 1.5 s Pune is moved to cell-3.

460
00:43:05.607 --> 00:43:09.063
Then every acknowledged item is read back through. the router.

461
00:43:09.343 --> 00:43:21.161
NAIVE ok 4,961 failed 0 acked items 4,961 MISSING 1,519 no hold worst. latency:

462
00:43:21.441 --> 00:43:24.044
Pune 20 ms Nagpur 18 ms.

463
00:43:24.324 --> 00:43:32.089
Pune items acknowledged before the move: ~1,515 Pune data left orphaned on. cell-1:

464
00:43:32.369 --> 00:43:33.095
YES.

465
00:43:33.375 --> 00:43:44.959
PROPER ok 7,264 failed 0 acked items 7,264 MISSING 0 Pune held 13 ms worst. latency:

466
00:43:45.239 --> 00:43:48.225
Pune 21 ms Nagpur 16 ms.

467
00:43:48.505 --> 00:43:56.953
Pune items acknowledged before the move: ~2,321 Pune data left orphaned on. cell-1: no.

468
00:43:57.233 --> 00:43:58.748
The rest is in the chapter.

469
00:44:01.598 --> 00:44:02.451
Reading it:

470
00:44:02.731 --> 00:44:18.795
The naive flip returns zero errors, and 1,519 acknowledged items are gone, matching the ~1,515 Pune items acknowledged before the move (the snapshot is taken an instant before the flip).

471
00:44:19.075 --> 00:44:23.171
Cell 3 has none of Pune's history; cell 1 still holds it, orphaned.

472
00:44:23.451 --> 00:44:25.307
Nothing in the error rate tells you.

473
00:44:25.587 --> 00:44:27.294
The proper move loses nothing.

474
00:44:27.574 --> 00:44:38.902
The router's per-city read or write lock holds only Pune's requests while in-flight ones drain, the data is copied, and the route flips; Nagpur, on the same source cell, is never held.

475
00:44:39.182 --> 00:44:44.515
The hold lasted ~13 ms here because Pune's data is a few thousand items.

476
00:44:45.865 --> 00:44:47.849
The hold grows with the tenant's data.

477
00:44:48.129 --> 00:45:02.999
For large cities, pre-copy with dual-write or CDC (Ch 28) so the hold covers only the final delta, verify counts or checksums before dropping the source, and replicate the routing table to every edge router.

478
00:45:04.349 --> 00:45:05.287
Part 6.

479
00:45:05.567 --> 00:45:06.847
Production Concerns.

480
00:45:08.097 --> 00:45:09.612
Failure modes by style.

481
00:45:09.892 --> 00:45:11.257
Here is a quick table:

482
00:45:11.537 --> 00:45:14.417
Style, and Characteristic failure, and Mitigation.

483
00:45:14.697 --> 00:45:15.871
Layered monolith.

484
00:45:16.151 --> 00:45:17.516
Characteristic failure:

485
00:45:17.796 --> 00:45:22.681
Tangled layers, rules in controllers or SQL, slow DB-bound tests.

486
00:45:22.961 --> 00:45:23.964
Mitigation:

487
00:45:24.244 --> 00:45:27.956
Hexagonal modules; enforce the dependency rule (L1).

488
00:45:28.236 --> 00:45:29.196
Hexagonal.

489
00:45:29.476 --> 00:45:30.841
Characteristic failure:

490
00:45:31.121 --> 00:45:34.641
Rule erodes via just this once infrastructure imports.

491
00:45:34.921 --> 00:45:35.924
Mitigation:

492
00:45:36.204 --> 00:45:39.233
ArchUnit or fitness functions in CI (L1).

493
00:45:39.513 --> 00:45:40.857
Modular monolith.

494
00:45:41.137 --> 00:45:42.503
Characteristic failure:

495
00:45:42.783 --> 00:45:46.004
Modules reach into each other's internals or tables.

496
00:45:46.284 --> 00:45:47.287
Mitigation:

497
00:45:47.567 --> 00:45:50.895
Spring Modulith verification; schema per module.

498
00:45:51.175 --> 00:45:52.455
Microservices.

499
00:45:52.735 --> 00:45:54.100
Characteristic failure:

500
00:45:54.380 --> 00:45:58.476
Synchronous chains: latency and availability multiply (L2).

501
00:45:58.756 --> 00:45:59.759
Mitigation:

502
00:46:00.039 --> 00:46:03.388
Events, fan-out, caching; extract only with a driver.

503
00:46:03.668 --> 00:46:04.692
Event-driven.

504
00:46:04.972 --> 00:46:06.337
Characteristic failure:

505
00:46:06.617 --> 00:46:09.391
Lost events, invisible flows, schema breaks.

506
00:46:09.671 --> 00:46:10.673
Mitigation:

507
00:46:10.953 --> 00:46:14.367
Outbox, tracing across async hops, schema registry.

508
00:46:15.717 --> 00:46:16.613
Serverless.

509
00:46:16.893 --> 00:46:18.258
Characteristic failure:

510
00:46:18.538 --> 00:46:21.055
Cold starts, runaway cost, time limits.

511
00:46:21.335 --> 00:46:22.338
Mitigation:

512
00:46:22.618 --> 00:46:27.098
SnapStart or native images, cost alerts, containers for steady load.

513
00:46:27.378 --> 00:46:28.167
Cells.

514
00:46:28.447 --> 00:46:29.813
Characteristic failure:

515
00:46:30.093 --> 00:46:34.167
Latent bug reaches every cell; router is a single point of failure.

516
00:46:34.447 --> 00:46:35.450
Mitigation:

517
00:46:35.730 --> 00:46:41.021
Slow waves + per-cell flags (L3); simple, replicated, well-tested router.

518
00:46:41.301 --> 00:46:42.261
Cell moves.

519
00:46:42.541 --> 00:46:43.906
Characteristic failure:

520
00:46:44.186 --> 00:46:47.002
Naive flip loses data silently (L4).

521
00:46:47.282 --> 00:46:48.285
Mitigation:

522
00:46:48.565 --> 00:46:52.319
Hold, copy, flip, verify, drop; CDC pre-copy.

523
00:46:53.669 --> 00:46:54.757
Observability.

524
00:46:55.037 --> 00:47:07.795
Tag every metric, log and trace with the cell ID (and context or module) so per-cell SLOs, dashboards and alerts exist; a global average hides a dead cell (Ch 24).

525
00:47:08.075 --> 00:47:13.792
Deploy-wave dashboards compare the newly deployed cell against the others (canary analysis).

526
00:47:14.072 --> 00:47:22.563
For modular monoliths, per-module metrics (latency, errors, DB time) keep the modules visible even though they share a process.

527
00:47:22.843 --> 00:47:31.717
For event-driven flows, distributed tracing across async hops (propagate trace context in event headers) makes choreography observable.

528
00:47:33.067 --> 00:47:34.006
Security.

529
00:47:34.286 --> 00:47:46.211
Hexagonal adapters are natural trust boundaries: validate and authenticate in driving adapters; keep secrets in driven adapters' configuration, never in the domain (Ch 23).

530
00:47:46.491 --> 00:47:54.043
Cells limit breach blast radius as well as outage blast radius when credentials, keys and databases are per cell.

531
00:47:54.323 --> 00:48:03.411
The cell router and control plane are high-value targets: least privilege, audited routing-table changes, and a signed, versioned routing table.

532
00:48:03.691 --> 00:48:10.113
BFFs must not become a bypass: authorization decisions stay in the owning service or context.

533
00:48:11.463 --> 00:48:12.828
Performance and tuning.

534
00:48:13.108 --> 00:48:24.137
Count required synchronous hops on critical paths and budget their latency (Ch 31 L3); L2 shows each hop's cost before any real network is involved.

535
00:48:24.417 --> 00:48:34.316
In modular monoliths, watch for module-level contention on shared pools (DB connections, thread pools), give heavy modules their own bulkhead pools.

536
00:48:34.596 --> 00:48:43.599
Cell sizing trades blast radius against efficiency: smaller cells mean smaller incidents but more fixed overhead and more cross-cell moves.

537
00:48:44.949 --> 00:48:51.711
Serverless: measure cold-start frequency and duration; provision concurrency for latency-sensitive paths.

538
00:48:53.061 --> 00:48:53.787
Cost.

539
00:48:54.067 --> 00:48:59.891
Hexagonal and modular monoliths are nearly free in infrastructure; their cost is discipline.

540
00:49:00.171 --> 00:49:06.848
Microservices add per-service fixed costs (compute baseline, pipelines, observability, on-call).

541
00:49:07.128 --> 00:49:18.435
Cells multiply the fixed infrastructure (every database, cluster and cache per cell), the grocery estimate was ~1.6x, and require a router and control plane.

542
00:49:18.715 --> 00:49:27.376
Justify them with the value of avoided platform-wide incidents (L3's 880, then 110 platform-minutes per year).

543
00:49:28.726 --> 00:49:36.790
Serverless is cheapest for spiky, low-duty-cycle work and most expensive for steady high volume (Ch 31 L4).

544
00:49:38.140 --> 00:49:39.079
Part 7.

545
00:49:39.359 --> 00:49:41.065
Anti-Patterns and Pitfalls.

546
00:49:42.315 --> 00:49:43.233
Watch out:

547
00:49:43.513 --> 00:49:44.153
1.

548
00:49:44.433 --> 00:49:46.353
Choosing one style for everything.

549
00:49:46.633 --> 00:49:48.510
We are a microservices company.

550
00:49:48.790 --> 00:49:53.782
Styles apply per level and per context; combine them (§2.11).

551
00:49:55.132 --> 00:49:56.049
Watch out:

552
00:49:56.329 --> 00:49:57.033
2.

553
00:49:57.313 --> 00:49:58.849
The distributed monolith.

554
00:49:59.129 --> 00:50:03.929
Services that must be deployed together and call each other synchronously in chains.

555
00:50:04.209 --> 00:50:08.028
All the costs of microservices (L2), none of the benefits.

556
00:50:09.378 --> 00:50:10.295
Watch out:

557
00:50:10.575 --> 00:50:11.322
3.

558
00:50:11.602 --> 00:50:13.394
Skipping the modular monolith.

559
00:50:13.674 --> 00:50:19.285
Going from a tangled monolith straight to services turns in-process coupling into network coupling.

560
00:50:19.565 --> 00:50:20.909
Modularize first.

561
00:50:22.259 --> 00:50:23.176
Watch out:

562
00:50:23.456 --> 00:50:24.160
4.

563
00:50:24.440 --> 00:50:25.976
Hexagonal in name only.

564
00:50:26.256 --> 00:50:32.763
Packages called domain that import JPA entities, HttpClient or Spring annotations.

565
00:50:33.043 --> 00:50:35.965
Enforce the dependency rule in CI (L1).

566
00:50:37.315 --> 00:50:38.233
Watch out:

567
00:50:38.513 --> 00:50:39.281
5.

568
00:50:39.561 --> 00:50:41.395
Ports that mirror the technology.

569
00:50:41.675 --> 00:50:49.014
A PaymentPort with postJson(url, body) isn't a port; it's an HTTP client with an interface.

570
00:50:49.294 --> 00:50:52.771
Ports speak the domain's language (charge(customer, amount)).

571
00:50:54.121 --> 00:50:55.039
Watch out:

572
00:50:55.319 --> 00:50:56.065
6.

573
00:50:56.345 --> 00:50:58.052
Long synchronous call chains.

574
00:50:58.332 --> 00:51:03.431
Nine required hops on checkout multiply latency and unavailability (L2).

575
00:51:03.711 --> 00:51:05.673
Fan out, cache, or use events.

576
00:51:07.023 --> 00:51:07.941
Watch out:

577
00:51:08.221 --> 00:51:08.967
7.

578
00:51:09.247 --> 00:51:12.917
Serverless for steady, latency-sensitive Java services.

579
00:51:13.197 --> 00:51:18.701
Cold starts and per-request pricing hurt exactly where steady containers are cheap and fast.

580
00:51:20.051 --> 00:51:20.968
Watch out:

581
00:51:21.248 --> 00:51:21.931
8.

582
00:51:22.211 --> 00:51:23.917
Cells with shared dependencies.

583
00:51:24.197 --> 00:51:34.523
A cell that still shares a global database, Kafka cluster or cache isn't a cell, §1.1's festival outage came through exactly those shared pools.

584
00:51:35.873 --> 00:51:36.790
Watch out:

585
00:51:37.070 --> 00:51:37.817
9.

586
00:51:38.097 --> 00:51:39.995
Fast deploy waves across cells.

587
00:51:40.275 --> 00:51:45.587
Rolling to all cells in 90 minutes gives no protection from latent bugs (L3).

588
00:51:45.867 --> 00:51:48.790
Bake times must match how long bugs take to show up.

589
00:51:50.140 --> 00:51:51.057
Watch out:

590
00:51:51.337 --> 00:51:52.041
10.

591
00:51:52.321 --> 00:51:54.284
Naive tenant moves between cells.

592
00:51:54.564 --> 00:52:01.391
Flipping the routing entry without moving data returns no errors and loses data silently (L4).

593
00:52:02.741 --> 00:52:03.658
Watch out:

594
00:52:03.938 --> 00:52:04.770
11.

595
00:52:05.050 --> 00:52:06.138
A smart router.

596
00:52:06.418 --> 00:52:13.629
Business logic, per-request database lookups or complex state in the cell router makes the one shared component fragile.

597
00:52:13.909 --> 00:52:15.658
Keep it dumb and replicated.

598
00:52:17.008 --> 00:52:17.925
Watch out:

599
00:52:18.205 --> 00:52:18.995
12.

600
00:52:19.275 --> 00:52:20.363
Cells too early.

601
00:52:20.643 --> 00:52:27.277
Cells for a 20-engineer product with one region multiply cost and tooling for a risk you don't yet have.

602
00:52:28.627 --> 00:52:29.502
Part 8.

603
00:52:29.782 --> 00:52:31.126
Interview Q and A.

604
00:52:32.376 --> 00:52:33.208
Beginner.

605
00:52:33.488 --> 00:52:34.512
Question 1.

606
00:52:34.792 --> 00:52:38.013
What is hexagonal (ports and adapters) architecture?

607
00:52:38.293 --> 00:52:39.659
Thirty-second answer:

608
00:52:39.939 --> 00:52:51.907
A way of structuring an application so the domain is at the center and depends on nothing; it defines ports, interfaces for what it needs (store orders, charge payments) and what it offers (use cases).

609
00:52:52.187 --> 00:53:00.635
Adapters implement those ports for specific technologies (JPA, Razorpay HTTP, REST controllers, Kafka listeners).

610
00:53:00.915 --> 00:53:09.640
Dependencies point inward, so the business logic can be tested without infrastructure and technologies can be swapped by writing new adapters.

611
00:53:10.990 --> 00:53:11.993
Question 2.

612
00:53:12.273 --> 00:53:13.958
What is a modular monolith?

613
00:53:14.238 --> 00:53:15.603
Thirty-second answer:

614
00:53:15.883 --> 00:53:31.179
A single deployable application divided internally into modules with enforced boundaries, typically one per bounded context, that communicate only through published module APIs or events, never through each other's internals or tables.

615
00:53:31.459 --> 00:53:39.033
It gives most of the modularity benefits of microservices with in-process calls, local transactions and one pipeline.

616
00:53:40.383 --> 00:53:41.428
Question 3.

617
00:53:41.708 --> 00:53:44.012
What is serverless and when is it a good fit?

618
00:53:44.292 --> 00:53:45.657
Thirty-second answer:

619
00:53:45.937 --> 00:53:55.580
You deploy functions or containers and the platform handles servers, scaling (including to zero) and patching; you pay per invocation and compute time.

620
00:53:55.860 --> 00:54:02.900
It fits bursty, stateless, event-triggered work, invoice PDFs, image resizing, webhooks, scheduled jobs.

621
00:54:03.180 --> 00:54:11.351
It fits poorly for steady high-volume, latency-sensitive services because of cold starts, time limits and linear per-request cost.

622
00:54:12.701 --> 00:54:13.767
Question 4.

623
00:54:14.047 --> 00:54:15.967
What is a cell-based architecture?

624
00:54:16.247 --> 00:54:17.613
Thirty-second answer:

625
00:54:17.893 --> 00:54:30.757
Running several complete, independent copies of the system's data plane, services, databases, queues, each serving a fixed subset of customers (for example, a group of cities), behind a thin router.

626
00:54:31.037 --> 00:54:40.551
Cells share nothing at runtime, so an infrastructure failure, a bad deploy or one tenant's surge affects only one cell's customers instead of everyone.

627
00:54:41.901 --> 00:54:42.904
Intermediate.

628
00:54:43.184 --> 00:54:44.293
Question 5.

629
00:54:44.573 --> 00:54:48.413
Layered versus hexagonal architecture, what's the actual difference?

630
00:54:48.693 --> 00:54:50.059
Thirty-second answer:

631
00:54:50.339 --> 00:54:52.920
The direction of the dependency at the bottom.

632
00:54:53.200 --> 00:55:01.499
In layered architecture the domain or service layer depends on the persistence layer, so business logic is shaped around the database.

633
00:55:01.779 --> 00:55:08.456
In hexagonal architecture the domain defines the persistence port and infrastructure depends on the domain.

634
00:55:08.736 --> 00:55:13.835
That inversion makes the domain testable without a database and technology-independent.

635
00:55:15.185 --> 00:55:16.294
Question 6.

636
00:55:16.574 --> 00:55:20.563
What does converting a module into a microservice actually cost?

637
00:55:20.843 --> 00:55:22.209
Thirty-second answer:

638
00:55:22.489 --> 00:55:43.481
Latency (a microsecond call becomes a network call; serial calls add up), tail latency (each hop adds its own p99), availability (required synchronous dependencies multiply unavailability), operations (pipelines, observability, on-call, versioning, contract tests), and the loss of local transactions.

639
00:55:43.761 --> 00:55:49.819
You pay it to get independent deployment, independent scaling, team autonomy and fault isolation.

640
00:55:51.169 --> 00:55:52.257
Question 7.

641
00:55:52.537 --> 00:55:56.548
Choreography versus orchestration in event-driven systems?

642
00:55:56.828 --> 00:55:58.193
Thirty-second answer:

643
00:55:58.473 --> 00:56:09.695
In choreography each service reacts to events and emits its own, with no central coordinator, loosely coupled but the overall flow is implicit and harder to follow and debug.

644
00:56:09.975 --> 00:56:22.967
In orchestration a coordinator (saga orchestrator, workflow engine) explicitly tells participants what to do, the flow is visible and error handling is centralized, at the cost of a central component.

645
00:56:24.317 --> 00:56:25.341
Question 8.

646
00:56:25.621 --> 00:56:28.714
What is a BFF and when would you use micro-frontends?

647
00:56:28.994 --> 00:56:30.359
Thirty-second answer:

648
00:56:30.639 --> 00:56:40.837
A Backend for Frontend is a thin backend per client type that aggregates and shapes data for that client, keeping client-specific logic out of domain services.

649
00:56:41.117 --> 00:56:48.669
Micro-frontends split a large web UI into independently built and deployed pieces owned by different teams.

650
00:56:48.949 --> 00:56:58.015
Use a BFF when clients have different needs; use micro-frontends only when several teams must deploy parts of one UI independently.

651
00:56:59.365 --> 00:57:00.432
Question 9.

652
00:57:00.712 --> 00:57:03.827
How do you enforce architecture style rules in code?

653
00:57:04.107 --> 00:57:05.472
Thirty-second answer:

654
00:57:05.752 --> 00:57:07.544
With fitness functions in CI:

655
00:57:07.824 --> 00:57:24.400
ArchUnit rules for layering and domain depends on nothing; Spring Modulith verification for module boundaries; dependency checks for cycles; per-module database schemas or grants so modules can't read each other's tables; and contract tests between services.

656
00:57:24.680 --> 00:57:26.323
Rules that aren't checked erode.

657
00:57:27.673 --> 00:57:28.441
Senior.

658
00:57:28.721 --> 00:57:29.766
Question 10.

659
00:57:30.046 --> 00:57:32.841
How do you decide cell size and the partition key?

660
00:57:33.121 --> 00:57:34.486
Thirty-second answer:

661
00:57:34.766 --> 00:57:42.681
Choose a key that keeps nearly all traffic within one cell, tenant, city group or customer ID, so cross-cell calls are rare.

662
00:57:42.961 --> 00:57:55.142
Size cells so losing one is tolerable (for example, at most 15% of customers), so the largest tenant fits in one cell with headroom, and so the number of cells is operationally manageable.

663
00:57:55.422 --> 00:57:57.193
Keep cells identical in shape.

664
00:57:58.543 --> 00:57:59.673
Question 11.

665
00:57:59.953 --> 00:58:04.476
Why didn't cells protect against latent bugs in L3, and how do you fix that?

666
00:58:04.756 --> 00:58:06.121
Thirty-second answer:

667
00:58:06.401 --> 00:58:17.068
Because a latent bug only triggers hours after deploy, and a 90-minute rollout has already put it into every cell by then, so the worst incident is still 100%.

668
00:58:17.348 --> 00:58:32.175
The fixes: space deploy waves by longer than typical latent-bug trigger times (at least for risky changes), use per-cell feature flags so behavior can be switched off where it misbehaves, and bake in the canary cell before continuing.

669
00:58:33.525 --> 00:58:34.613
Question 12.

670
00:58:34.893 --> 00:58:37.261
How do you move a tenant between cells safely?

671
00:58:37.541 --> 00:58:38.906
Thirty-second answer:

672
00:58:39.186 --> 00:58:52.306
Hold the tenant's requests at the router and let in-flight ones drain; copy its data from the source cell to the target; flip the routing entry; release the hold; verify counts or checksums; then drop the source data.

673
00:58:52.586 --> 00:58:58.687
For large tenants, pre-copy with dual-write or CDC so the hold covers only the final delta.

674
00:59:00.037 --> 00:59:01.317
Question 13.

675
00:59:01.597 --> 00:59:04.989
What belongs in a cell's control plane versus data plane?

676
00:59:05.269 --> 00:59:06.635
Thirty-second answer:

677
00:59:06.915 --> 00:59:11.821
The data plane serves customer requests: the router and everything inside cells.

678
00:59:12.101 --> 00:59:20.869
The control plane manages them: cell provisioning, the routing table source of truth, deploy-wave orchestration, tenant moves, capacity.

679
00:59:21.149 --> 00:59:30.515
The data plane must keep working if the control plane is down, cells serve with the last good routing table, so the control plane can be simpler and less available.

680
00:59:31.865 --> 00:59:33.059
Question 14.

681
00:59:33.339 --> 00:59:35.067
When is serverless the wrong choice?

682
00:59:35.347 --> 00:59:36.713
Thirty-second answer:

683
00:59:36.993 --> 00:59:53.867
For steady, high-volume, latency-sensitive services (cold starts and per-request pricing hurt), long-running or stateful work (time limits, statelessness), workloads needing specialized runtimes or tuning, and teams that need strong local reproducibility.

684
00:59:54.147 --> 01:00:01.422
For Java, heavy frameworks worsen cold starts unless you use SnapStart or CRaC or native images.

685
01:00:02.772 --> 01:00:03.668
Architect.

686
01:00:03.948 --> 01:00:05.143
Question 15.

687
01:00:05.423 --> 01:00:09.476
Design the architecture-style mix for a large grocery platform.

688
01:00:09.756 --> 01:00:11.121
Thirty-second answer:

689
01:00:11.401 --> 01:00:24.991
Per level: hexagonal inside every deployable; bounded contexts as modules in a modular monolith where they change together (Ordering, Catalog), extracted into services where there's a driver (Inventory,

690
01:00:25.271 --> 01:00:40.247
Fulfilment for flash-sale scaling); events between contexts; BFF per client; serverless for bursty side jobs; and, at large scale, cells by city group with a replicated router and a separate control plane, deployed in waves.

691
01:00:41.597 --> 01:00:42.791
Question 16.

692
01:00:43.071 --> 01:00:45.653
How would you evolve a tangled layered monolith?

693
01:00:45.933 --> 01:00:47.298
Thirty-second answer:

694
01:00:47.578 --> 01:00:49.519
Don't jump to microservices.

695
01:00:49.799 --> 01:00:59.975
First draw bounded contexts (Ch 33) and restructure into a modular monolith with hexagonal modules, enforcing boundaries with fitness functions.

696
01:01:00.255 --> 01:01:05.525
Then extract only the modules with real drivers, using the strangler pattern and events.

697
01:01:05.805 --> 01:01:09.047
Add cells when blast radius becomes the dominant risk.

698
01:01:10.397 --> 01:01:11.741
Question 17.

699
01:01:12.021 --> 01:01:16.971
How do you handle cross-cell requirements like national reporting and global uniqueness?

700
01:01:17.251 --> 01:01:18.616
Thirty-second answer:

701
01:01:18.896 --> 01:01:20.688
Avoid live cross-cell calls.

702
01:01:20.968 --> 01:01:28.072
For reporting, stream each cell's changes (CDC or events) into a central analytics store and query there.

703
01:01:28.352 --> 01:01:35.264
For uniqueness, use IDs that embed the cell (or globally unique IDs like UUIDv7 or Snowflake).

704
01:01:35.544 --> 01:01:43.096
For globally shared data (catalog, pricing rules), replicate read-only copies into each cell from a global source.

705
01:01:43.376 --> 01:01:46.704
Customers who move cities need a tenant-move protocol.

706
01:01:48.054 --> 01:01:49.227
Question 18.

707
01:01:49.507 --> 01:01:52.665
Microservices versus cells, aren't they the same thing?

708
01:01:52.945 --> 01:01:54.310
Thirty-second answer:

709
01:01:54.590 --> 01:01:56.979
No, they partition along different axes.

710
01:01:57.259 --> 01:02:07.819
Microservices split the system by function (ordering, inventory), so each service serves all customers; a service failure affects everyone who needs that function.

711
01:02:08.099 --> 01:02:15.651
Cells split by customer or tenant, each cell running every function; a cell failure affects only that cell's customers.

712
01:02:15.931 --> 01:02:19.259
They combine: each cell can contain several services.

713
01:02:20.609 --> 01:02:21.847
Question 19.

714
01:02:22.127 --> 01:02:24.324
How do you justify cells to finance?

715
01:02:24.604 --> 01:02:25.969
Thirty-second answer:

716
01:02:26.249 --> 01:02:27.615
Quantify both sides.

717
01:02:27.895 --> 01:02:37.644
Cost: duplicated infrastructure per cell (~1.6x for the grocery platform), router and control-plane engineering, operational tooling.

718
01:02:37.924 --> 01:02:42.404
Benefit: expected reduction in platform-wide outage minutes (L3:

719
01:02:42.684 --> 01:02:58.641
880, then 110 per year), times the cost of an outage minute (lost orders, refunds, reputation), plus the tail risk, worst incident 100%, then 12.5%, which matters most during festival peaks.

720
01:02:59.991 --> 01:03:01.101
Question 20.

721
01:03:01.381 --> 01:03:06.117
What distinguishes a principal engineer's answer to 'what architecture should we use?'.

722
01:03:06.397 --> 01:03:07.762
Thirty-second answer:

723
01:03:08.042 --> 01:03:09.919
It depends, and here's on what.

724
01:03:10.199 --> 01:03:27.159
They identify the drivers per part of the system, choose a style per level, quantify the costs of each boundary (latency, availability, operations, infrastructure), name the evolution path and the triggers for each next step, and make sure every style's rules are enforced.

725
01:03:27.439 --> 01:03:29.445
They also say what they'd not do yet.

726
01:03:30.795 --> 01:03:31.691
Part 9.

727
01:03:31.971 --> 01:03:33.379
Summary and Practice.

728
01:03:34.629 --> 01:03:35.717
Key takeaways.

729
01:03:35.997 --> 01:03:46.151
Styles are bundles of constraints that buy specific qualities; choose per level and per context, not per company (§2.1, §2.11).

730
01:03:46.431 --> 01:03:53.962
Hexagonal architecture inverts the dependency so the domain owns its ports; enforce the rule in CI (L1).

731
01:03:54.242 --> 01:04:03.074
The modular monolith is the default: cheap calls, simple ops, and cheap later extraction if boundaries are enforced (§2.4).

732
01:04:03.354 --> 01:04:11.461
Microservices have a measurable price, latency, tail, availability, operations, pay it only for a named driver (L2).

733
01:04:12.811 --> 01:04:21.323
Events decouple contexts; choose choreography or orchestration by how much visibility the flow needs (§2.6).

734
01:04:21.603 --> 01:04:28.259
Serverless fits bursty, stateless work, not steady latency-sensitive services (§2.7).

735
01:04:28.539 --> 01:04:36.901
Cells contain blast radius for infrastructure failures and noisy neighbors, and for bad deploys only with slow enough waves (L3).

736
01:04:37.181 --> 01:04:42.771
Tenant moves between cells need a protocol; naive flips lose data silently (L4).

737
01:04:44.121 --> 01:04:52.121
Evolve: layered, then modular (hexagonal), then selective services, then cells, each step keeping the previous one's discipline.

738
01:04:53.471 --> 01:04:54.793
Decision cheat sheet.

739
01:04:55.073 --> 01:04:56.439
Here is a quick table:

740
01:04:56.719 --> 01:04:57.807
Need, and Style.

741
01:04:58.087 --> 01:05:01.009
Testable business rules, swappable infrastructure:

742
01:05:01.289 --> 01:05:03.337
Hexagonal inside the deployable.

743
01:05:03.617 --> 01:05:06.881
Several contexts that change together, one team or a few:

744
01:05:07.161 --> 01:05:08.505
Modular monolith.

745
01:05:08.785 --> 01:05:12.391
A context with a different scaling profile, cadence or owner:

746
01:05:12.671 --> 01:05:15.103
Extract to a service; connect with events.

747
01:05:15.383 --> 01:05:17.687
Multi-step process with compensation:

748
01:05:17.967 --> 01:05:20.441
Orchestrated saga or workflow engine.

749
01:05:20.721 --> 01:05:22.791
Fan-out of facts to many consumers:

750
01:05:23.071 --> 01:05:24.649
Events (choreography).

751
01:05:24.929 --> 01:05:26.892
Bursty, stateless side jobs:

752
01:05:27.172 --> 01:05:28.452
Serverless functions.

753
01:05:28.732 --> 01:05:30.033
Different client needs:

754
01:05:30.313 --> 01:05:31.700
BFF per client.

755
01:05:31.980 --> 01:05:33.751
Many teams on one web UI:

756
01:05:34.031 --> 01:05:35.268
Micro-frontends.

757
01:05:36.618 --> 01:05:40.949
Contain infra failures, noisy tenants, bad deploys at large scale:

758
01:05:41.229 --> 01:05:44.301
Cells + router + control plane + slow waves.

759
01:05:45.651 --> 01:05:46.781
Exercises.

760
